window.OS_SEARCH = [{"t":"Getting started","h":"","u":"docs/getting-started.html","x":"OpenShell consists of two binaries:"},{"t":"Getting started","h":"1. Install","u":"docs/getting-started.html#1-install","x":"OpenShell consists of two binaries: openshell – the CLI (also runs the headless runtime and the MCP server) openshell-desktop – the desktop shell (Tauri 2) Prebuilt binaries (from the releases page ): curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RobinsSuperSchmiede/EDA/main/scripts/install.sh | sh # macOS / Linux irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RobinsSuperSchmiede/EDA/main/scripts/install.ps1 | iex # Windows (PowerShell) From source (needed until the first release is published, and for development): git clone https://github.com/RobinsSuperSchmiede/EDA.git cd EDA cargo build --release Put target/release on your PATH (both binaries must be in the same directory, or set OPENSHELL_DESKTOP "},{"t":"Getting started","h":"2a. New project","u":"docs/getting-started.html#2a-new-project","x":"openshell create my-app # HTML/JS + Python stdlib, no installs openshell create my-app --template react-python # React (Vite) + Python openshell create --list # show all templates cd my-app openshell dev"},{"t":"Getting started","h":"2b. Existing project","u":"docs/getting-started.html#2b-existing-project","x":"cd my-existing-project openshell init # detects frontend (Vite, Next, CRA, Angular, Vue CLI, static) and backend (Python, Node, Go, Rust) openshell doctor # verifies commands, directories, ports openshell dev init writes a starting point – review the generated openshell.json , especially ports and health checks. A typical configuration: { \"version\": 1, \"app\": { \"name\": \"My App\" }, \"frontend\": { \"command\": \"npm run dev -- --port ${port} --strictPort\", \"cwd\": \"frontend\", \"port\": \"auto\", \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:${port}\", \"setup\": { \"command\": \"npm install\", \"creates\": \"frontend/node_modules\" }, \"build\": \"npm run build\", \"dist\": \"frontend/dist\", \"proxy\": { \"/api\": \"${services.backend.url}\" }, \""},{"t":"Getting started","h":"3. Run","u":"docs/getting-started.html#3-run","x":"Command What happens openshell dev Development mode: window, file watching, broad agent permissions, logs in the terminal. openshell dev --headless Same without a window – open the printed URL in your browser (browser mode). openshell start Production mode (uses frontend.dist when configured). openshell start --headless --detach Background runtime without window. openshell status / logs -f / restart backend / stop Manage the running app from another terminal. While openshell dev runs: edit backend files → the backend restarts ( watch ), edit frontend files → the dev server hot-reloads, or OpenShell reloads the window for static frontends, a crashing service is restarted with backoff; after 5"},{"t":"Getting started","h":"4. Connect an AI agent","u":"docs/getting-started.html#4-connect-an-ai-agent","x":"Coding agents: every project created with openshell create / openshell init contains an AGENTS.md , and openshell guide prints the complete guide for building OpenShell apps. To connect an agent to the running app: claude mcp add my-app-dev -- openshell mcp --project /path/to/my-app # develop the app claude mcp add my-app -- openshell mcp --app --start --project /path/to/my-app # use the app See Agent Interaction Layer for other agents, the tool list and permissions."},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"","u":"docs/tutorial.html","x":"In about 20 minutes you build Bookmarks, a small desktop app with a Python backend and a plain HTML frontend, make it usable for AI agents and package it as an installer. Everything here works the sam…"},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"1. Create the project","u":"docs/tutorial.html#1-create-the-project","x":"openshell create bookmarks cd bookmarks openshell dev A window opens with the template's todo list. The terminal shows the logs of both services: backend │ starting: python main.py backend │ ready after 0.4s frontend │ serving …/web at http://127.0.0.1:53121/ Leave it running. The project looks like this: openshell.json what OpenShell runs (open it – it is short) backend/main.py JSON API, Python standard library web/ index.html, app.js, style.css – served by OpenShell, /api is proxied to the backend AGENTS.md instructions for AI coding agents (see step 6)"},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"2. Add a backend endpoint","u":"docs/tutorial.html#2-add-a-backend-endpoint","x":"Replace the todo logic in backend/main.py with bookmarks. The essentials of the OpenShell contract are already in the template: PORT = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"8000\")) # OpenShell assigns the port DATA_DIR = os.environ.get(\"OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR\") or \"../data\" # per-app data directory # ... if path == \"/health\": # health check used by OpenShell return self.send_json(200, {\"status\": \"ok\"}) Add two routes (sketch): if path == \"/api/bookmarks\" and self.command == \"GET\": q = parse_qs(urlparse(self.path).query).get(\"q\", [\"\"])[0].lower() items = [b for b in load() if q in b[\"title\"].lower() or q in b[\"url\"].lower()] return self.send_json(200, items) if path == \"/api/bookmarks\" and self.command == "},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"3. Update the frontend","u":"docs/tutorial.html#3-update-the-frontend","x":"Edit web/index.html and web/app.js to list GET /api/bookmarks and post new ones. On save, OpenShell reloads the window ( \"watch\": [\"web\"] in the config). With a dev server template (React/Vue/Svelte) the dev server hot-reloads instead. Useful while developing: openshell status # state, pid, port, CPU, memory of each service openshell logs -f # follow all logs openshell restart backend Right-click the window's title bar (Windows) or use the tray icon for the same overview."},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"4. Make the app usable for AI agents","u":"docs/tutorial.html#4-make-the-app-usable-for-ai-agents","x":"Agents should be able to use Bookmarks without clicking through the UI. Declare its functions as capabilities in openshell.json : \"capabilities\": [ { \"name\": \"search_bookmarks\", \"description\": \"Search bookmarks by text in title or URL\", \"permission\": \"read\", \"input_schema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"q\": { \"type\": \"string\" } } }, \"http\": { \"service\": \"backend\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"path\": \"/api/bookmarks\" } }, { \"name\": \"add_bookmark\", \"description\": \"Save a bookmark\", \"permission\": \"actions\", \"input_schema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"title\": { \"type\": \"string\" }, \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\" } }, \"required\": [\"title\", \"url\"] }, \"http\": { \"service\": \"backend\", \"method\": \"POST\""},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"5. Connect an assistant","u":"docs/tutorial.html#5-connect-an-assistant","x":"claude mcp add bookmarks -- openshell mcp --app --start --project /path/to/bookmarks Ask: \"Save https://modelcontextprotocol.io as 'MCP spec' in my bookmarks.\" The assistant sees exactly app_status , search_bookmarks and add_bookmark – nothing else. With --start , the app is started in the background if it is not running. To let a coding agent work on the app, connect the full profile instead: openshell mcp --project … ( Agent Interaction Layer )."},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"6. Let an AI coding agent extend the app","u":"docs/tutorial.html#6-let-an-ai-coding-agent-extend-the-app","x":"The project contains an AGENTS.md (and CLAUDE.md ) that tells coding agents how OpenShell apps work, how to verify changes ( openshell status , openshell call ui_snapshot , openshell logs --errors ) and how to expose new functions. Open the folder in Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, … and ask: Add folders to the bookmarks app: each bookmark can be in a folder, the UI shows a folder list, and agents can list folders and move bookmarks."},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"7. Ship it","u":"docs/tutorial.html#7-ship-it","x":"Give the app a version and an icon in openshell.json : \"app\": { \"name\": \"Bookmarks\", \"id\": \"com.example.bookmarks\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"icon\": \"icon.png\" } openshell start # try production mode first openshell package # → .openshell/package/bin/bundle/nsis/Bookmarks_1.0.0_x64-setup.exe (Windows) The installed app stores its data in the user's data directory, runs in production mode (agents get only read , ui and actions by default) and can be used by agents with bookmarks.exe --mcp . More in Installers and releases ."},{"t":"Tutorial: build a complete OpenShell app","h":"Where to go next","u":"docs/tutorial.html#where-to-go-next","x":"Configuration reference – databases as external services, workers, tasks, proxies, modes Languages – Java/Spring Boot, C++/CMake, Go, Rust, .NET backends Agents – permissions, approvals, UI automation, audit log"},{"t":"Building blocks: build only what you need","h":"","u":"docs/building-blocks.html","x":"An OpenShell app is assembled from four independent building blocks. Every block is optional – use one, several or all of them:"},{"t":"Building blocks: build only what you need","h":"Typical combinations","u":"docs/building-blocks.html#typical-combinations","x":"You want Blocks How it works A desktop UI with local storage frontend + database The page uses OpenShell.db.query/execute from the SDK . An AI interface to your data database + functions Agents get your functions plus db_query / db_execute (as allowed by agent_access ). Tools for AI agents only functions Each function is a script: JSON in, JSON out. The app has no UI and no server. A background service backend Runs headless or in the tray ( \"window\": {\"enabled\": false} ). A classic web app as desktop app frontend + backend (+ database) As before; the backend can use OPENSHELL_DB_MAIN . Apps without a frontend show OpenShell's console in the window: services, databases (with a data browser), "},{"t":"Building blocks: build only what you need","h":"Frontend","u":"docs/building-blocks.html#frontend","x":"\"frontend\": { \"dist\": \"web\", \"watch\": [\"web\"] } Any frontend works – static files, a Vite/Next dev server ( command + url ) or an external URL; see Configuration . The starter page from openshell add frontend adapts to the other blocks: it shows a backend call, a notes list from the database and the app's functions."},{"t":"Building blocks: build only what you need","h":"Backend","u":"docs/building-blocks.html#backend","x":"{ \"name\": \"backend\", \"command\": [\"python\", \"main.py\"], \"cwd\": \"backend\", \"port\": \"auto\", \"healthcheck\": \"http://127.0.0.1:${port}/health\", \"watch\": [\"backend\"] } Any language ( Languages ). Read PORT , bind 127.0.0.1 , answer /health ."},{"t":"Building blocks: build only what you need","h":"Database","u":"docs/building-blocks.html#database","x":"\"databases\": [ { \"name\": \"main\", \"migrations\": \"db/migrations\", \"agent_access\": \"write\" } ] File : <name>.db in the app's data directory ( OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR , per user for installed apps). Override with \"path\" (relative to the data directory, or absolute). Migrations : every *.sql file in migrations is applied once, in file-name order, inside a transaction. Add new files ( 002_add_tags.sql ) – never edit applied ones. Services and functions get the path as OPENSHELL_DB_<NAME> (e.g. OPENSHELL_DB_MAIN ) and the main database as DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///… . Any SQLite library works (Python sqlite3 , better-sqlite3 , JDBC, …). Frontend : OpenShell.db.query(sql, params) / OpenShell.db.execute(sql,"},{"t":"Building blocks: build only what you need","h":"Functions","u":"docs/building-blocks.html#functions","x":"\"capabilities\": [ { \"name\": \"summarize_notes\", \"description\": \"Summarize all open notes\", \"permission\": \"read\", \"input_schema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"limit\": { \"type\": \"integer\" } } }, \"run\": [\"python\", \"tools/summarize_notes.py\"] } ] import json, os, sqlite3, sys args = json.load(sys.stdin) # input object con = sqlite3.connect(os.environ[\"OPENSHELL_DB_MAIN\"]) rows = con.execute(\"SELECT title FROM notes WHERE done = 0 LIMIT ?\", (args.get(\"limit\", 20),)).fetchall() print(json.dumps({\"open\": [r[0] for r in rows]})) # result Input: JSON object on stdin (also in OPENSHELL_INPUT ). Output: JSON or text on stdout. Errors: message on stderr and a non-zero exit code. \"run\": {\"command\""},{"t":"Languages: C++, Java and others","h":"","u":"docs/languages.html","x":"OpenShell runs any executable, so every language works. Compiled languages use a build step:"},{"t":"Languages: C++, Java and others","h":"Java","u":"docs/languages.html#java","x":""},{"t":"Languages: C++, Java and others","h":"Plain JDK (template vanilla-java)","u":"docs/languages.html#plain-jdk-template-vanilla-java","x":"{ \"name\": \"backend\", \"cwd\": \"backend\", \"build\": [\"javac\", \"-d\", \"build/classes\", \"src/Main.java\"], \"command\": [\"java\", \"-cp\", \"build/classes\", \"Main\"], \"port\": \"auto\", \"healthcheck\": \"http://127.0.0.1:${port}/health\", \"watch\": [\"backend/src\"] }"},{"t":"Languages: C++, Java and others","h":"Spring Boot","u":"docs/languages.html#spring-boot","x":"{ \"name\": \"backend\", \"cwd\": \"backend\", \"command\": [\"mvnw\", \"-q\", \"spring-boot:run\", \"-Dspring-boot.run.arguments=--server.port=${port}\"], \"port\": \"auto\", \"healthcheck\": { \"http\": \"http://127.0.0.1:${port}/actuator/health\", \"ready_timeout_ms\": 120000 }, \"openapi\": \"/v3/api-docs\", \"watch\": [\"backend/src\"] } mvnw / gradlew are found in the service directory on all platforms. With springdoc-openapi the openapi field turns every REST endpoint into an agent tool. For production use the jar: \"build\": { \"command\": [\"mvnw\", \"-q\", \"-DskipTests\", \"package\"], \"on_start\": \"never\" }, \"command\": [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"target/app-1.0.0.jar\", \"--server.port=${port}\"] Gradle: [\"gradlew\", \"-q\", \"bootRun\", \"--args=-"},{"t":"Languages: C++, Java and others","h":"C++","u":"docs/languages.html#c","x":""},{"t":"Languages: C++, Java and others","h":"CMake (template vanilla-cpp)","u":"docs/languages.html#cmake-template-vanilla-cpp","x":"{ \"name\": \"backend\", \"cwd\": \"backend\", \"build\": \"cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && cmake --build build --config Release\", \"command\": [\"build/bin/todo-server\"], \"port\": \"auto\", \"watch\": [\"backend/src\", \"backend/CMakeLists.txt\"] } command paths without extension also find name.exe on Windows. Multi-config generators (Visual Studio) put binaries into build/Release/ – the template sets CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY so the path is build/bin everywhere. On Windows run OpenShell from a Developer PowerShell (or install CMake + Ninja / MinGW) so the compiler is found. Ship only the binary: \"package\": { \"include\": [\"openshell.json\", \"web\", \"backend/build/bin\"] } . HTTP libraries such "},{"t":"Languages: C++, Java and others","h":"Others","u":"docs/languages.html#others","x":"Language build command Rust cargo build --release [\"target/release/server\"] Go go build -o bin/server . [\"bin/server\"] (or go run . without build) C# / .NET dotnet build -c Release [\"dotnet\", \"run\", \"--no-build\", \"-c\", \"Release\", \"--\", \"--urls\", \"http://127.0.0.1:${port}\"] Kotlin (Ktor) – [\"gradlew\", \"-q\", \"run\"] Python – ( setup for pip install ) [\"python\", \"main.py\"] Node.js / Deno / Bun – [\"node\", \"server.js\"] , [\"deno\", \"run\", \"-A\", \"main.ts\"] ASP.NET (Swashbuckle), FastAPI, NestJS and Spring all publish OpenAPI documents – set openapi and agents can use the backend without UI automation."},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"","u":"docs/configuration.html","x":"OpenShell looks for openshell.json or openshell.toml in the given directory and its parents. All relative paths are resolved against the directory containing the file. Unknown fields are errors (typos…"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"Top level","u":"docs/configuration.html#top-level","x":"Field Type Description version number Schema version, currently 1 (default). Newer versions are rejected with a clear message. app object name (required), id , version (semver, needed for installers), description , icon (PNG for window and installers). window object Native window settings. frontend object What the window shows. services array Backends, databases, workers, AI servers, … databases array Built-in SQLite databases managed by OpenShell. runtime object Runtime manager behaviour. agents object Agent Interaction Layer: permissions, approvals, providers. capabilities array Functions the app exposes to agents (HTTP endpoint, script, task). tasks object Named commands ( \"test\": \"pytest"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"window","u":"docs/configuration.html#window","x":"Field Default Description title app.name Window title width , height 1280, 800 Initial size min_width , min_height – Minimum size (both required to take effect) resizable true maximized false close_to_tray false Closing hides the window; services keep running title_bar \"native\" \"overlay\" : the frontend draws the title bar (see Frontend SDK ). No system title bar on Windows/Linux; window buttons over the content on macOS tray true Show a tray icon (show, reload, runtime panel, restart services, quit) devtools dev mode only Allow WebView devtools enabled true false : no window – the app runs in the tray / background (services, databases, agent functions). Apps without a frontend show OpenShell"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"frontend","u":"docs/configuration.html#frontend","x":"OpenShell does not care which framework you use. It needs one of: command + url (or port ): a process that serves the frontend (dev server, local web server), url alone: an already running or remote frontend, dist : a directory of built files that OpenShell serves itself. Field Description command Shell string ( \"npm run dev\" ) or argv array cwd Working directory env Extra environment variables (values support ${...} ) port Number or \"auto\" ; available as ${port} and $PORT url URL loaded into the window dist Built frontend directory (production) build Build command for openshell build setup One-time step, e.g. { \"command\": \"npm install\", \"creates\": \"frontend/node_modules\" } proxy Static serv"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"services[]","u":"docs/configuration.html#services","x":"Field Default Description name – Unique, [a-z0-9_-] ( frontend is reserved) description , kind – Informational ( backend , database , worker , ai , …) command – Shell string or argv. In argv form python falls back to python3 (and vice versa) args [] Appended arguments cwd project root env {} Values support ${...} port – Number or \"auto\" ; exported as PORT url http://127.0.0.1:<port> Base URL (used by ${services.<name>.url} and HTTP capabilities) external false Do not start; only monitor ( url / healthcheck required) healthcheck TCP on port URL string (HTTP 2xx/3xx) or { \"http\" | \"tcp\", \"interval_ms\", \"timeout_ms\", \"failure_threshold\", \"ready_timeout_ms\" } restart true (on-failure) true , fal"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"Variables","u":"docs/configuration.html#variables","x":"Usable in commands, args, env values, URLs, health checks and proxy targets: Variable Value ${port} Port of the current service / frontend ${services.<name>.port} / ${services.<name>.url} Another service ${frontend.url} Frontend URL ${project.root} Absolute project directory ${mode} development / production ${env.NAME} Environment variable of the shell $$ Literal $"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"runtime","u":"docs/configuration.html#runtime","x":"Field Default Description autostart true Start services when the shell starts background_mode false Keep running in the tray when the window is closed control_port random Fixed port for the control API log_buffer_lines 10000 Lines kept in memory (files in .openshell/logs/ ) shutdown_timeout_ms 5000 Default grace period when stopping launch_at_login false Start the installed app when the user logs in (installed apps only) remote off Remote access for other devices – see runtime.remote"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"runtime.remote","u":"docs/configuration.html#runtimeremote","x":"Makes the app usable from other devices (a laptop using an app hosted on a phone, an agent on another machine). Everything except enabled is optional. Full guide: Remote access . Field Default Description enabled false Start the gateway (app profile over MCP) and the exposed ports bind 0.0.0.0 Address to listen on; a VPN address (Tailscale 100.x.y.z ) limits access to your devices port 7420 Gateway port allow everyone IP addresses / CIDR ranges that may connect, e.g. [\"192.168.1.0/24\", \"100.64.0.0/10\"] . Loopback is always allowed shared_token true Accept the shared pairing token; false = only per-device tokens ( openshell remote pair <device> ) rate_limit 120 / 10 / 5 { \"requests_per_minute"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"agents","u":"docs/configuration.html#agents","x":"Field Default Description enabled true Disable to deny all agent tools permissions dev: read, ui, actions, services, files, code · prod: read, ui, actions A list for all modes or { \"development\": [...], \"production\": [...] } approval_required [\"system\"] Scopes that need a human decision approval_timeout_ms 120000 Unanswered requests are denied sdk_permissions [\"read\", \"ui\"] Scopes for the frontend SDK (app token) providers [] { \"name\", \"kind\": \"ollama\" | \"lm-studio\" | \"openai-compatible\" | \"openai\" | \"anthropic\" | \"mcp\" | \"custom\", \"url\", \"api_key_env\", \"models\" } detect_local_providers true Probe Ollama (11434) and LM Studio (1234) Permission scopes: read , ui , actions (alias write ), serv"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"capabilities[]","u":"docs/configuration.html#capabilities","x":"{ \"name\": \"add_todo\", \"description\": \"Create a todo\", \"permission\": \"actions\", \"input_schema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"title\": { \"type\": \"string\" } }, \"required\": [\"title\"] }, \"http\": { \"service\": \"backend\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"/api/todos\" } } Exactly one target: http – service + path , or url ; GET/DELETE send the input as query parameters, other methods as JSON body. run – a script/program: [\"python\", \"tools/x.py\"] or { \"command\", \"cwd\", \"env\", \"timeout_ms\" } . It receives the input as JSON on stdin (and in OPENSHELL_INPUT ) and prints its result (JSON or text) on stdout; a non-zero exit code is an error (message from stderr). No server needed – see Building blocks . ta"},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"databases[]","u":"docs/configuration.html#databases","x":"{ \"name\": \"main\", \"migrations\": \"db/migrations\", \"agent_access\": \"write\" } Field Default Description name – [a-z0-9_] ; services get the path as OPENSHELL_DB_<NAME> type \"sqlite\" Engine (SQLite is built in) path <name>.db File, relative to the app's data directory ( OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR ) or absolute migrations – Folder of *.sql files, applied once each in file-name order agent_access \"read\" none , read ( db_list , db_schema , db_query ) or write (also db_execute ) description – Shown to agents The main (or only) database is also exported as DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///… . Details: Building blocks ."},{"t":"Configuration reference (openshell.json, version 1)","h":"TOML","u":"docs/configuration.html#toml","x":"version = 1 [app] name = \"My App\" [frontend] command = \"npm run dev\" cwd = \"frontend\" url = \"http://localhost:5173\" [[services]] name = \"api\" command = [\"node\", \"server.js\"] port = 3001 restart = \"always\""},{"t":"CLI reference","h":"","u":"docs/cli.html","x":"Global option: -p, --project <dir> – project directory (default: current directory or the nearest parent with an openshell.json). Environment: OPENSHELL_LOG (log filter, e.g. debug), OPENSHELL_DESKTOP…"},{"t":"CLI reference","h":"Project setup","u":"docs/cli.html#project-setup","x":"Command Description openshell create <name> [-t <template>] New project from a template ( vanilla-python default, react-python , react-node , vue-python , svelte-go , vanilla-java , vanilla-cpp , custom ). --list shows all. openshell create <name> --with frontend,backend,database,functions [--lang python|node] New project composed of building blocks . Without --template / --with the terminal asks. openshell add frontend | backend [--lang …] | database [name] | function <name> [--lang …] Add a building block to the current app (files + openshell.json ). openshell init [--name <n>] [--toml] [--force] Generate a config for an existing project (auto-detection) and an AGENTS.md for coding agents "},{"t":"CLI reference","h":"Running","u":"docs/cli.html#running","x":"Command Description openshell dev [--headless] Development mode. Opens the desktop window (or runs headless for the browser) and streams logs. Ctrl+C stops everything. openshell start [--headless] [--detach] Production mode. --detach returns immediately and keeps the app running in the background. openshell stop Stop the whole shell (all services, window). openshell stop <service> Stop one service. openshell run <service> Start a stopped service (and its dependencies). openshell restart <service> Restart a service and wait until it is healthy."},{"t":"CLI reference","h":"Inspecting","u":"docs/cli.html#inspecting","x":"Command Description openshell status [--json] Mode, URLs, services (state, pid, port, CPU, memory, restarts, errors), bridged pages, pending approvals. openshell logs [service] [-f] [-n 100] [--errors] [--grep <text>] Logs of all or one service. openshell audit [-n 30] Recent agent actions. openshell remote Remote gateway: URLs, MCP endpoint, TLS fingerprint, allowlist, exposed services, paired devices, blocked addresses, shared token and an MCP client configuration ( runtime.remote ). openshell remote pair <device> [--no-qr] Create a token for one device (shown once) with a QR code / pairing URI and links to exposed services. openshell remote devices Paired devices with last use. openshell "},{"t":"CLI reference","h":"Agents","u":"docs/cli.html#agents","x":"Command Description openshell mcp [--app] [--start] MCP server over stdio. --app : only the application's own functions (agents that use the app). --start : start the app headless if it is not running. openshell tools [--json] Tools with scope and current access (allowed / approval / denied). openshell call <tool> ['<json args>'] Call a tool as agent cli , e.g. openshell call ui_click '{\"text\":\"Save\"}' . openshell approvals Pending approval requests. openshell approve <id> / openshell deny <id> Answer an approval request."},{"t":"Frontend SDK (@openshell/sdk)","h":"","u":"docs/sdk.html","x":"The SDK is optional. Your frontend works unchanged inside and outside OpenShell; the SDK adds access to the runtime, the native window and the agent layer when available."},{"t":"Frontend SDK (@openshell/sdk)","h":"Loading","u":"docs/sdk.html#loading","x":"The shell serves the SDK at {control_url}/sdk.js , so no package installation is needed: async function openshell() { if (!window.__OPENSHELL__) return null; // plain browser const { OpenShell } = await import(/* @vite-ignore */ `${window.__OPENSHELL__.api}/sdk.js`); return OpenShell; } Or copy packages/sdk into your project / install it from the repository ( npm install <path>/packages/sdk ) and import { OpenShell } from \"@openshell/sdk\" ."},{"t":"Frontend SDK (@openshell/sdk)","h":"API","u":"docs/sdk.html#api","x":"OpenShell.available: boolean // running inside OpenShell? OpenShell.mode: \"development\" | \"production\" | null OpenShell.host: \"desktop\" | \"browser\" | null await OpenShell.info() // app, mode, host, granted SDK permissions, service names await OpenShell.runtime.status() // read await OpenShell.runtime.logs({ service: \"backend\", limit: 50 }) // read await OpenShell.runtime.restart(\"backend\") // services await OpenShell.runtime.start(\"worker\") / .stop(\"worker\") // services await OpenShell.window.minimize() / maximize() / unmaximize() / show() / hide() / focus() // ui await OpenShell.notify(\"Export finished\", \"Saved to reports/q3.pdf\") // ui await OpenShell.providers() // read – configured and l"},{"t":"Frontend SDK (@openshell/sdk)","h":"Exposing frontend actions to agents","u":"docs/sdk.html#exposing-frontend-actions-to-agents","x":"OpenShell.agent.registerAction( \"open_invoice\", { description: \"Open the invoice editor for an invoice id\", permission: \"ui\", input_schema: { type: \"object\", properties: { id: { type: \"string\" } }, required: [\"id\"] }, }, async ({ id }) => { router.push(`/invoices/${id}`); return { opened: id }; }, ); Agents discover it with capabilities_list and call it as cap_open_invoice or app_invoke . The handler runs in the page; its return value (JSON) is sent back to the agent."},{"t":"Frontend SDK (@openshell/sdk)","h":"Own title bar","u":"docs/sdk.html#own-title-bar","x":"With \"window\": { \"title_bar\": \"overlay\" } the desktop window has no system title bar (Windows, Linux) or shows only the window buttons over the content (macOS), and the frontend draws its own. The desktop window provides: const win = window.__OPENSHELL__?.window; // undefined in a plain browser if (win?.title_bar === \"overlay\") { win.os; // \"windows\" | \"macos\" | \"linux\" – macOS: leave ~80px on the left for its buttons await win.minimize(); await win.toggleMaximize(); await win.close(); // respects close_to_tray await win.isMaximized(); await win.showMenu(); // the runtime menu (services, logs, reload) at the cursor } Mark the bar with data-tauri-drag-region=\"deep\" : dragging it moves the win"},{"t":"Control API","h":"","u":"docs/control-api.html","x":"Every running shell exposes a local HTTP API on 127.0.0.1:<port>. Its URL and admin token are written to <project>/.openshell/runtime.json while the shell runs:"},{"t":"Control API","h":"Endpoints","u":"docs/control-api.html#endpoints","x":"Method & path Token Description GET /v1/health – { ok, app, mode, host, platform, version } GET /sdk.js , GET /bridge.js – Frontend SDK, UI bridge script GET /v1/status (alias /v1/info ) admin App, mode, host, platform capabilities, pid, URLs, services, bridged pages, pending approvals, agent permissions, in_background , keep_alive , remote POST /v1/services/{name}/{start|stop|restart} admin Change the desired state (returns immediately) GET /v1/logs?service=&since=&limit=&contains=&errors_only= admin { lines: [{ seq, ts, service, stream, text }], last_seq } POST /v1/shutdown admin Stop all services and exit GET /v1/approvals admin { pending: [{ id, agent, tool, permission, summary, args, cr"},{"t":"Control API","h":"Example","u":"docs/control-api.html#example","x":"URL=$(jq -r .url .openshell/runtime.json); TOKEN=$(jq -r .token .openshell/runtime.json) curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" $URL/v1/status curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" -H \"X-OpenShell-Agent: my-script\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\ -d '{\"service\":\"backend\",\"errors_only\":true}' $URL/v1/tools/logs_read"},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"","u":"docs/installers.html","x":"openshell package turns an OpenShell project into a native installer – without writing or compiling any desktop code for your app."},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"What happens","u":"docs/installers.html#what-happens","x":"openshell build runs (skip with --skip-build ): compiled services ( services[].build , e.g. Maven, CMake) and the frontend ( frontend.build ). The application files are staged into app/ (see Which files are shipped ). The generic openshell-desktop binary is copied, renamed to your app ( my-app.exe ) and re-branded with your icon ( app.icon , PNG – OpenShell's icon if unset). No compiler is involved. The Tauri bundler produces the installer(s): Platform Formats ( --target / package.targets ) Default Windows nsis (setup.exe, per-user install, no admin rights), msi nsis macOS app , dmg app , dmg Linux deb , rpm , appimage deb , appimage The bundler downloads its tools (NSIS, WiX, appimagetool) "},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"Installed app behaviour","u":"docs/installers.html#installed-app-behaviour","x":"%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\My App\\ (Windows, NSIS per-user install) my-app.exe the shell, with your icon app\\openshell.json your application app\\... Starts in production mode (agent permissions from agents.permissions.production ). State lives in a per-user directory named after the bundle identifier, e.g. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\com.example.myapp\\ (Windows), ~/Library/Application Support/com.example.myapp/ (macOS), ~/.local/share/com.example.myapp/ (Linux): logs, audit log, runtime.json and data/ , which services get as OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR . Store your database/files there so they survive updates and uninstalls. Single instance: starting the app again focuses the running window. A background instance st"},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"Configuration","u":"docs/installers.html#configuration","x":"{ \"app\": { \"name\": \"My App\", \"id\": \"com.example.myapp\", \"version\": \"1.2.0\", \"icon\": \"assets/icon.png\" }, \"package\": { \"publisher\": \"Example Inc.\", \"homepage\": \"https://example.com\", \"copyright\": \"© 2026 Example Inc.\", \"license_file\": \"LICENSE\", \"targets\": [\"nsis\", \"msi\"], \"exclude\": [\"docs\", \"**/*.test.js\"], \"include\": [] } } app.version must be a semantic version ( 1.2.0 ). package.identifier overrides app.id ."},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"Which files are shipped","u":"docs/installers.html#which-files-are-shipped","x":"By default the whole project except .git , .openshell , node_modules , __pycache__ , *.pyc , virtualenvs, .gradle , IDE folders. Add exclude patterns (globs, relative to the project), or list exactly what to ship with include (the config file is always included): \"package\": { \"include\": [\"openshell.json\", \"web\", \"backend/build/bin\"] } // C++: only the compiled binary \"package\": { \"exclude\": [\"backend/src\"] } // Java: ship classes, not sources \"package\": { \"exclude\": [\"frontend/src\", \"frontend/public\"] } // ship only frontend/dist"},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"Runtimes","u":"docs/installers.html#runtimes","x":"The installer contains the shell and your app files. Interpreters and runtimes your services need – Python, Node.js, a JRE – must exist on the target machine (or be shipped by you inside the app, e.g. a jlink runtime image or an embedded Python, referenced by a relative command ). Compiled services (C++, Rust, Go, GraalVM native images) need nothing extra. If a runtime is missing, the loading screen shows the error ( java was not found in PATH ). Bundled runtimes are on the roadmap ."},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"Portable app bundle (.osapp)","u":"docs/installers.html#portable-app-bundle-osapp","x":"openshell package --target bundle # dist/my-app-1.2.0.osapp – works on every platform, also in Termux A bundle is a zip with the shipped files (same include / exclude rules) and openshell-bundle.json (app, identifier, version). It runs unchanged on every OpenShell host: Host How Android app open or share the .osapp with OpenShell ( Android app ) Termux, servers, desktops with the CLI openshell install my-app-1.2.0.osapp --start openshell install unpacks into ~/.openshell/apps/<identifier>/ ( OPENSHELL_APPS_DIR ); installing a newer bundle replaces the files and keeps the app's data ( .openshell/ ). openshell apps lists installed bundles."},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"One link per system","u":"docs/installers.html#one-link-per-system","x":"A developer builds once, users download one file for their system – without changing the program. openshell release workflow # writes .github/workflows/openshell-release.yml git tag v1.2.0 && git push origin v1.2.0 On every tag the workflow builds in parallel and publishes a GitHub release with: File For My App_1.2.0_x64-setup.exe Windows (NSIS, per-user install) My App_1.2.0_aarch64.dmg , My App_1.2.0_x64.dmg macOS Apple Silicon / Intel my-app_1.2.0_amd64.AppImage , .deb Linux my-app-1.2.0.osapp Android (OpenShell app, Termux), servers, any host downloads.json machine-readable list: platform, arch, URL, size, SHA-256 index.html download page that offers the visitor's system first https://gi"},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"Update checks","u":"docs/installers.html#update-checks","x":"\"package\": { \"update_url\": \"https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/latest/download/downloads.json\" } openshell update and the control API ( GET /v1/update ) compare the running version with the newest release and return the right download for the current system. Installing updates automatically (download, verify, replace) is planned."},{"t":"Installers (openshell package)","h":"Signing","u":"docs/installers.html#signing","x":"Unsigned installers trigger SmartScreen / Gatekeeper warnings. Configure signing in openshell.json ; secrets stay in the environment or the certificate store: \"package\": { \"signing\": { \"windows\": { \"certificate_thumbprint\": \"A1B2…\", \"timestamp_url\": \"http://timestamp.digicert.com\" }, \"macos\": { \"signing_identity\": \"Developer ID Application: Example Inc. (TEAMID)\" } } } Platform Setting Notes Windows certificate_thumbprint Certificate in the user's/machine's store (import a .pfx first); SHA-256 digest Windows sign_command Any signer, e.g. Azure Trusted Signing or a cloud HSM: [\"trusted-signing-cli\", \"-e\", \"…\", \"%1\"] ( %1 = file) Windows timestamp_url RFC 3161 timestamp server, keeps signature"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"","u":"docs/platforms.html","x":"OpenShell is not tied to the desktop. The runtime, the agent layer, the control API and the configuration are the same everywhere; a small platform adapter decides how programs are started and how the…"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Goals","u":"docs/platforms.html#goals","x":"One core, several platforms. openshell-core runs unchanged on Windows, macOS, Linux, headless servers and Android. Platform code lives in adapters and hosts, not in the core. The phone as a personal runtime and mini server. Apps, services, databases, automations and agents generated by AI run locally on the phone and stay reachable for your other devices. AI builds, the user does not edit code. Agents create, extend, test and manage apps through the same MCP tools as on the desktop; the platform tells them which building blocks exist there. Connected devices. Desktop, phone and server are meant to manage and develop projects together (see device mesh )."},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Layers","u":"docs/platforms.html#layers","x":"┌───────────────────────── hosts ─────────────────────────┐ │ desktop app (Tauri) headless CLI Android app (Kotlin) │ │ window, tray, dialogs terminal Activity + WebView, │ │ foreground service │ └──────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬──────────────┘ │ Host trait │ lifecycle events ┌──────────────▼──────────────────────────────▼──────────────┐ │ openshell-core: Shell, control API, MCP, remote gateway │ │ openshell-agent: policy, approvals, audit, UI bridge │ │ openshell-runtime: plan, supervisor, health, logs, static │ │ server, databases, lifecycle │ └──────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ RuntimeAdapter ┌──────────────▼──────────────────────────────────────"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Capabilities","u":"docs/platforms.html#capabilities","x":"Every runtime reports what its platform offers. Agents see it in app_info and GET /v1/status : \"platform\": { \"platform\": \"android\", \"os\": \"android\", \"arch\": \"aarch64\", \"processes\": \"restricted\", \"background\": \"foreground_service\", \"file_watching\": true, \"app_window\": true, \"tray\": false, \"installers\": false, \"toolchains\": false, \"wake_lock\": true } Field Desktop Server Android app Termux processes full full restricted – system tools and bundled programs full background always always foreground_service foreground_service (wake lock) app_window / tray yes / yes no / no yes / no no / no (browser) installers yes no no no ( .osapp bundles yes) toolchains – build steps, dev servers, npm / pip / ca"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Configuration","u":"docs/platforms.html#configuration","x":"{ \"services\": [ { \"name\": \"compiler\", \"command\": \"cargo run\", \"platforms\": [\"desktop\", \"server\"] }, { \"name\": \"sync\", \"command\": [\"node\", \"sync.mjs\"], \"background\": \"pause\" } ], \"runtime\": { \"remote\": { \"enabled\": true, \"bind\": \"0.0.0.0\", \"port\": 7420 } } } services[].platforms – only run on these platforms. Like modes , services for other platforms are left out of the plan and dependencies on them are dropped. services[].background – keep (default) or pause : stop while the app is in the background, start again when it returns. Only matters on platforms with background: foreground_service . runtime.remote – the remote gateway ."},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Android","u":"docs/platforms.html#android","x":""},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"What runs on Android","u":"docs/platforms.html#what-runs-on-android","x":"Android allows an app to execute only files inside its APK (the native library directory) and system tools – files written at runtime cannot be executed (W^X, Android 10+). There is no PATH with toolchains. The AndroidAdapter follows these rules and turns violations into a clear error that tells the developer or the agent what to use instead: `cargo` cannot run on Android: apps may only start system tools and programs shipped with the app (bundled: node). Use a static frontend, the built-in database or functions for a bundled interpreter, or limit this service with \"platforms\": [\"desktop\", \"server\"] Building block On Android Static frontend ( frontend.dist ) ✅ built-in static server Built-in"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Termux: apps on the phone","u":"docs/platforms.html#termux-apps-on-the-phone","x":"Termux is a Linux environment for Android with its own package manager. The OpenShell CLI runs there and detects it ( $PREFIX / $TERMUX_VERSION ), so the phone becomes a development machine and a portable host – no computer needed: curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RobinsSuperSchmiede/EDA/main/scripts/install.sh | sh pkg install python nodejs openjdk-17 rust golang postgresql # what your apps need openshell create notes --with frontend,backend,database cd notes && openshell dev # dev server, file watching, agents – like on the desktop openshell start --headless # production; open the URL in the phone's browser openshell package --target bundle # notes-0.1.0.osapp for the Android a"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Lifecycle, battery and network","u":"docs/platforms.html#lifecycle-battery-and-network","x":"Android stops apps in the background. The host forwards the Activity and system events: Android LifecycleEvent Effect onStart foreground paused services start again onStop background services with \"background\": \"pause\" stop onTrimMemory(RUNNING_CRITICAL) low_memory pausable services stop PowerManager.isPowerSaveMode power_save reported to agents and hosts ConnectivityManager callback network reported ( connected , metered ) Runtime::keep_alive() lists the active services that must keep running ( background: keep ). While it is not empty the Android host keeps a foreground service (type specialUse or dataSync , with a persistent notification) alive and asks the user once to exempt the app fro"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Status","u":"docs/platforms.html#status","x":"✅ The core and the CLI build for aarch64-linux-android and x86_64-linux-android (CI checks the Android build on every push). ✅ Verified on an Android 16 emulator: openshell dev --headless serves a static frontend, migrates the database, runs functions through /system/bin/sh , leaves desktop-only services out, pauses and resumes services on lifecycle events and serves the app profile to a laptop through the remote gateway. ✅ Termux: TermuxAdapter with toolchains, wake lock and pkg install hints; prebuilt CLI for aarch64-linux-android in releases, install.sh detects Termux. 🚧 Android app: Kotlin host + JNI bridge in apps/android / crates/android , AAB build for the Play Store in CI – see Andro"},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"The Android app","u":"docs/platforms.html#the-android-app","x":"The OpenShell Android app is a host around the unchanged core – see Android app : Shell app – Kotlin Activity with a WebView (no JavaScript bridge for app pages) and the core as a JNI library ( libopenshell_android.so ). Foreground service ( specialUse ) – keeps the runtime alive, shows a notification with the running services, forwards lifecycle, memory and network events, partial wake lock. App manager – install apps from .osapp bundles (file picker, share sheet), open, stop. Bundled runtimes (planned) – Node.js and Python as lib*.so in the APK, registered automatically when present. On-device agents – remote agents through the gateway today; local models planned."},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Remote access","u":"docs/platforms.html#remote-access","x":"The remote gateway makes an app usable from other devices – a laptop agent using functions of an app hosted on the phone, a browser opening a website the phone serves, a desktop tool using a database on the phone. Off by default; tokens per device, an address allowlist, rate limits with lockout, TLS, opt-in exposure per service and an audit trail can be combined: \"runtime\": { \"remote\": { \"enabled\": true, \"allow\": [\"192.168.1.0/24\"], \"tls\": { \"enabled\": true }, \"expose\": [{ \"service\": \"frontend\", \"port\": 8080 }] } } openshell remote pair laptop # token for one device, QR code, links openshell remote # URLs, fingerprint, devices, blocked addresses Full guide: Remote access ."},{"t":"Platforms: desktop, server and Android","h":"Device mesh (in progress)","u":"docs/platforms.html#device-mesh-in-progress","x":"Desktop, phone and server running OpenShell should form one personal mesh: ✅ Pairing – per-device tokens with a QR code / pairing URI ( openshell remote pair ), TLS with pinned self-signed certificates, revocation per device. Discovery – mDNS ( _openshell._tcp ) in the local network, VPN addresses otherwise. Remote control API – manage services, logs and approvals of another device's apps with the same CLI ( openshell --device phone status ). Project sync – projects as folders synchronised between devices (git-based), so an agent can develop on the desktop and deploy to the phone ( openshell deploy --device phone ). Placement – services[].platforms already lets one project describe what runs"},{"t":"Remote access: use an app from other devices","h":"","u":"docs/remote-access.html","x":"An OpenShell app can be reached from your other devices over IP address and port – for example a phone that hosts an app, its database and its API, and a laptop (or an AI agent on it) that uses them.…"},{"t":"Remote access: use an app from other devices","h":"What other devices can reach","u":"docs/remote-access.html#what-other-devices-can-reach","x":"Port What Login runtime.remote.port (7420) The gateway : GET /v1/health (public), GET /v1/remote/info and POST /mcp/app – the app's own functions over MCP, never developer tools, files, logs or the admin API token expose[].port , protocol: http A service's website or API, forwarded unchanged (WebSockets, streaming and uploads work) token (default) or none expose[].port , protocol: tcp Any TCP protocol (PostgreSQL, Redis, MQTT, a game server …), bytes forwarded unchanged address allowlist only The local control API ( 127.0.0.1 , admin token) is never reachable from other devices."},{"t":"Remote access: use an app from other devices","h":"Security layers","u":"docs/remote-access.html#security-layers","x":"Layer Setting Notes Off by default enabled Nothing listens until you switch it on Bind address bind 0.0.0.0 = every network; a VPN address ( 100.x.y.z for Tailscale) = only your devices Opt-in per service expose[] A service is reachable only if you list it, on the port you choose Address allowlist allow , expose[].allow IPs or CIDR ranges; others are dropped right after the TCP handshake. Loopback is always allowed Tokens shared token, openshell remote pair <device> Per-device tokens are stored as SHA-256 hashes, revocable one by one; shared_token: false accepts only them Rate limit rate_limit.requests_per_minute Per address: requests on the gateway, new connections on exposed ports Lockout "},{"t":"Remote access: use an app from other devices","h":"Pair a device","u":"docs/remote-access.html#pair-a-device","x":"openshell remote pair laptop prints the device's token (only once), the MCP endpoint, the TLS fingerprint, a link per exposed HTTP service and a QR code with a pairing URI: openshell://pair?app=Notes&url=https%3A%2F%2F192.168.1.20%3A7420&token=osd_…&fp=AB%3ACD%3A… Use the token as Authorization: Bearer <token> (MCP clients, scripts) or open https://192.168.1.20:8080/?openshell_token=<token> once in a browser: OpenShell sets an HttpOnly , SameSite=Strict cookie and reloads the page without the token in the address bar. openshell remote devices # paired devices, last use openshell remote revoke laptop # the laptop's token stops working immediately openshell remote # everything at a glance MCP "},{"t":"Remote access: use an app from other devices","h":"TLS","u":"docs/remote-access.html#tls","x":"\"tls\": { \"enabled\": true } creates a self-signed certificate once ( .openshell/data/remote-cert.pem , remote-key.pem ) for localhost , the device's addresses and reuses it. Browsers warn about self-signed certificates; compare the fingerprint that openshell remote shows with the one the browser displays, or use your own certificate: \"tls\": { \"enabled\": true, \"cert\": \"certs/fullchain.pem\", \"key\": \"certs/privkey.pem\" } With Tailscale, tailscale cert <name>.ts.net gives a trusted certificate for the device."},{"t":"Remote access: use an app from other devices","h":"Typical setups","u":"docs/remote-access.html#typical-setups","x":"Goal Configuration Phone hosts an app, laptop at home uses it bind: \"0.0.0.0\" , allow: [\"192.168.1.0/24\"] , tls.enabled , pair the laptop Reachable only through a VPN bind: \"100.x.y.z\" (the phone's Tailscale address), shared_token: false Database on the phone for a desktop tool expose: [{ \"service\": \"postgres\", \"port\": 5433, \"protocol\": \"tcp\", \"allow\": [\"192.168.1.10\"] }] A website for the family in the LAN expose: [{ \"service\": \"frontend\", \"port\": 8080 }] , open the link from openshell remote pair <device> Never expose a port to the internet without TLS and an allowlist; prefer a VPN."},{"t":"Remote access: use an app from other devices","h":"Limits","u":"docs/remote-access.html#limits","x":"Exposed HTTP ports check the token on the first request of a connection ; later requests on the same keep-alive connection are trusted. After revoking a device, its open connections end when the client closes them (restart the app to cut them immediately). tcp ports have no login – use allow , a VPN, or the service's own authentication. Rate limits and blocks are kept in memory and reset when the app restarts. The tokens give access to the app profile and exposed services only. Per-device permissions (different functions per device) are planned."},{"t":"The OpenShell Android app","h":"","u":"docs/android-app.html","x":"apps/android/ is the native Android host: it runs OpenShell apps on the phone with the unchanged core, keeps them alive with a foreground service and can be published in the Google Play Store."},{"t":"The OpenShell Android app","h":"How it works","u":"docs/android-app.html#how-it-works","x":"The user adds an app bundle ( .osapp , built with openshell package --target bundle ) from the file picker or the share sheet. It is unpacked into the app's private storage ( files/apps/<identifier>/ ); its data lives in files/state/<identifier>/ and survives updates. Opening the app starts RuntimeService (foreground service, type specialUse ) which calls NativeRuntime.start(…) . The core runs with an AndroidAdapter that allows the APK's native library directory and registers bundled runtimes ( libnode.so → node , libpython.so → python ) when the APK contains them. AppActivity shows the frontend from http://127.0.0.1:<port> in a WebView without any JavaScript bridge to Android, and forwards "},{"t":"The OpenShell Android app","h":"Build","u":"docs/android-app.html#build","x":"Requirements: JDK 17, Android SDK (API 36), Android NDK, Rust with rustup target add aarch64-linux-android x86_64-linux-android , and cargo-ndk ( cargo install cargo-ndk ). # 1. the Rust core as libopenshell_android.so, 16 KB page aligned (required for Android 15+ / Play) RUSTFLAGS=\"-C link-arg=-Wl,-z,max-page-size=16384\" \\ cargo ndk --platform 26 -t arm64-v8a -t x86_64 -o apps/android/app/src/main/jniLibs \\ build --release -p openshell-android # 2. APK for testing and AAB for the Play Store cd apps/android gradle assembleRelease bundleRelease # Gradle 8.14 (or add a wrapper: `gradle wrapper`) adb install app/build/outputs/apk/release/app-release.apk CI does the same in .github/workflows/and"},{"t":"The OpenShell Android app","h":"Signing","u":"docs/android-app.html#signing","x":"Google Play uses Play App Signing : you sign the AAB with an upload key , Google re-signs for distribution. keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore upload.jks -alias upload -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 10000 cp apps/android/keystore.properties.example apps/android/keystore.properties # fill in, never commit In CI set the secrets ANDROID_KEYSTORE (base64 of upload.jks ), ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD , ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS , ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD . Without them the release build is unsigned."},{"t":"The OpenShell Android app","h":"Publishing in the Play Store – checklist","u":"docs/android-app.html#publishing-in-the-play-store--checklist","x":"What this repository prepares and what the account owner has to do: Step Prepared here You do Developer account – Register at play.google.com/console (one-time fee, identity verification) App and package name dev.openshell.android Create the app in the console (change the ID first if you publish under your own name) Signed AAB CI workflow, keystore.properties Create the upload key, add the secrets, enable Play App Signing Target API level targetSdk 36 Keep it current every year (Play deadlines usually end of August) 16 KB page size linker flag + CI check – Store listing store/listing.md Add icon (512 px), feature graphic (1024 × 500), screenshots Privacy policy store/privacy-policy.md (templ"},{"t":"The OpenShell Android app","h":"Status and open points","u":"docs/android-app.html#status-and-open-points","x":"✅ JNI bridge ( crates/android ) with tests on the desktop; builds for aarch64-linux-android in CI. ✅ Kotlin host: launcher, app view, foreground service, notification, wake lock, network and lifecycle events, bundle import. 🚧 Not yet verified on a device in this round (the Gradle build runs in CI; no Android SDK on the development machine). ⏳ Bundled runtimes ( libnode.so , libpython.so ): need prebuilt Android binaries and their licences in the APK. ⏳ Approvals on the phone (agent asks for permission): the core supports it; the app has no dialog yet. ⏳ Pairing by scanning a QR code ( openshell://pair?... ) and using apps hosted on other devices. ⏳ Tauri mobile shell: the plain Kotlin host w"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"","u":"docs/agents.html","x":"OpenShell gives AI agents a standardized, model-agnostic interface to a running desktop application – in two profiles:"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Connecting","u":"docs/agents.html#connecting","x":""},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Using an installed app","u":"docs/agents.html#using-an-installed-app","x":"Installed apps (see Installers and releases ) are MCP servers themselves: { \"mcpServers\": { \"my-app\": { \"command\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\me\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Programs\\\\My App\\\\my-app.exe\", \"args\": [\"--mcp\"] } } } If the app is not running, it starts in the background without a window , so an agent can use it while the user does something else. Opening the app later replaces the background instance with the window. The tool list contains only the app's functions; permissions follow agents.permissions.production . Add --profile full for the developer tool set. The runtime panel (title bar right-click → Runtime panel ) shows both commands ready to copy."},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Using a project from the CLI","u":"docs/agents.html#using-a-project-from-the-cli","x":"openshell mcp --app --start --project /path/to/app # app profile, start headless (production) if needed"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Developing: MCP over stdio (recommended)","u":"docs/agents.html#developing-mcp-over-stdio-recommended","x":"openshell mcp --project /path/to/app # add --start to launch `openshell dev --headless` on demand Configure it as an MCP server in your client: claude mcp add my-app -- openshell mcp --project /path/to/app # Claude Code { \"mcpServers\": { \"my-app\": { \"command\": \"openshell\", \"args\": [\"mcp\", \"--project\", \"/path/to/app\"] } } } The stdio server forwards to the running shell and reconnects automatically, so the agent can be started before the app. If the app is not running, tools/list still works and tool calls explain how to start it."},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"MCP over HTTP","u":"docs/agents.html#mcp-over-http","x":"POST {control_url}/mcp (full profile) and POST {control_url}/mcp/app (app profile) implement the Streamable HTTP transport (JSON responses). They require Authorization: Bearer <token> from .openshell/runtime.json . Because the port and token change on each start, stdio is more convenient; set runtime.control_port for a fixed port."},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"From another device (remote gateway)","u":"docs/agents.html#from-another-device-remote-gateway","x":"With \"runtime\": { \"remote\": { \"enabled\": true } } the app profile is also reachable from other devices – for example an agent on a laptop using an app hosted on a phone. openshell remote prints the URL and a ready MCP client configuration ( { \"url\": \"http://<ip>:7420/mcp/app\", \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <pairing token>\" } } ). Only the app's functions are exposed; see Platforms ."},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Plain HTTP / JSON","u":"docs/agents.html#plain-http--json","x":"GET /v1/tools list tools with permission and access (allowed / approval / denied) POST /v1/tools/{name} body = arguments → { \"ok\": true, \"result\": ... } or { \"ok\": false, \"result\": { \"error\": \"...\" } } Send X-OpenShell-Agent: <name> to identify the agent in the audit log. From a terminal: openshell call <tool> '<json>' ."},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Tools","u":"docs/agents.html#tools","x":"Tool Scope Purpose app_info read Overview: app, mode, services, permissions, capabilities, hints. Call first. runtime_status read State, pid, port, URL, restarts, last error per service logs_read read Log lines ( service , since , limit , contains , errors_only ) capabilities_list read Capabilities from config and frontend config_get read Effective configuration providers_list read Configured and detected AI providers with models audit_read read Recent agent actions ui_pages ui Pages connected to the UI bridge ui_snapshot ui Title, URL, headings, dialogs, text and interactive elements with refs ui_click ui Click by ref , selector or text ui_fill ui Set input/textarea/select/checkbox/contente"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Resources and notifications","u":"docs/agents.html#resources-and-notifications","x":"Besides tools, the MCP server offers read-only resources : openshell://status , openshell://config , openshell://logs , openshell://capabilities (developer profile), openshell://app (app profile) and openshell://db/<name>/schema for every database agents may read. openshell mcp (stdio) sends notifications/tools/list_changed whenever the tool list changes – for example when the frontend registers an action, an OpenAPI document is imported or the app is started after the agent connected."},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"UI automation","u":"docs/agents.html#ui-automation","x":"ui_snapshot returns a compact, semantic view of the page: { \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:52471/\", \"title\": \"Todo App\", \"headings\": [{ \"level\": 1, \"text\": \"Todo App\" }], \"elements\": [ { \"ref\": \"e1\", \"role\": \"textbox\", \"name\": \"New todo\", \"value\": \"\" }, { \"ref\": \"e2\", \"role\": \"button\", \"name\": \"Add\" }, { \"ref\": \"e6\", \"role\": \"checkbox\", \"name\": \"Buy milk\", \"checked\": false } ], \"text\": \"Todo App 3 todos · 1 done …\", \"actions\": [{ \"name\": \"set_filter\", \"description\": \"…\" }] } Use the refs with ui_click {\"ref\": \"e2\"} or ui_fill {\"ref\": \"e1\", \"value\": \"…\", \"submit\": true} . Refs stay stable while the element exists; take a new snapshot after larger UI changes. The bridge ( bridge.js ) is injected int"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Capabilities: the application's own functions","u":"docs/agents.html#capabilities-the-applications-own-functions","x":"Prefer capabilities over UI automation – they are faster and more reliable, and they are what the app profile exposes. Three sources: OpenAPI import – set \"openapi\": \"/openapi.json\" (FastAPI), \"/v3/api-docs\" (Spring/springdoc), \"/swagger/v1/swagger.json\" (ASP.NET) or a file/URL on a service. When the service becomes healthy, every operation becomes a tool: name from operationId , input schema from path/query parameters and the JSON request body, GET → scope read , other methods → actions . Filter with { \"location\": \"...\", \"include\": [...], \"exclude\": [...], \"prefix\": \"shop\" } . Declared in openshell.json ( capabilities ), backed by an HTTP endpoint, a script ( run – JSON in on stdin, JSON ou"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Permissions and approvals","u":"docs/agents.html#permissions-and-approvals","x":"Every tool needs exactly one scope: Scope Allows Default dev Default prod read state, logs, config, capabilities list ✅ ✅ ui UI automation, window control, notifications ✅ ✅ actions capabilities that change data ✅ ✅ services start/stop/restart services ✅ – files read project files ✅ – code write project files ✅ – system declared tasks, JavaScript evaluation – – Configure with agents.permissions . Scopes in agents.approval_required (default [\"system\"] ) need a human decision: the desktop app shows a dialog (and the runtime panel lists pending requests), headless mode prints the request; answer with openshell approve <id> / openshell deny <id> . Unanswered requests are denied after approval_ti"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"Security","u":"docs/agents.html#security","x":"The control API listens on 127.0.0.1 only and requires a bearer token. The admin token lives in .openshell/runtime.json (mode 0600 on Unix, ignored by git). Requests with a foreign Host header (DNS rebinding) or a non-allow-listed Origin (other websites) are rejected. The frontend gets a separate app token limited to /v1/sdk/* and the bridge, with agents.sdk_permissions . Application frontends loaded from http(s) URLs get no Tauri IPC access. File tools reject absolute paths, .. , symlink escapes and OpenShell's state directory; fs_write also protects .git . Agents cannot run arbitrary shell commands; only tasks declared in the config, under the system scope. The remote gateway (off by defau"},{"t":"Agent Interaction Layer","h":"A typical agent loop","u":"docs/agents.html#a-typical-agent-loop","x":"app_info → understand the app, its services and capabilities. Edit code with the agent's own file tools (or fs_write ). Services with watch restart automatically; otherwise service_restart . Check runtime_status / logs_read {\"errors_only\": true} . ui_reload (static frontends reload automatically), then ui_snapshot and ui_click / ui_fill to verify the feature like a user would."},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"","u":"docs/ai.html","x":"OpenShell is designed to be worked on by AI agents in three ways. This folder contains the material that makes AI harnesses productive with it."},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"How the repository supports AI harnesses","u":"docs/ai.html#how-the-repository-supports-ai-harnesses","x":"File Read by Purpose /AGENTS.md Codex, Cursor, Aider, Jules, Zed, … Entry point: building an app vs. working on OpenShell; project map, commands, conventions /CLAUDE.md Claude Code Imports AGENTS.md /.github/copilot-instructions.md GitHub Copilot Pointer to the same material /llms.txt LLMs / tools following llmstxt.org Index of the documentation /llms-full.txt LLMs with large context All relevant docs in one file (generated by scripts/gen-llms-full.py ) app-development.md coding agents The complete guide for building apps (embedded in the CLI as openshell guide ) skills/openshell/SKILL.md Claude Code / Agent SDK skills Installable skill AGENTS.md + CLAUDE.md in every new project coding agent"},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"Setup recipes","u":"docs/ai.html#setup-recipes","x":""},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"Claude Code","u":"docs/ai.html#claude-code","x":"# make the skill available in all projects mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills/openshell cp docs/ai/skills/openshell/SKILL.md ~/.claude/skills/openshell/ # connect a running app (develop) or an app to use claude mcp add my-app-dev -- openshell mcp --project /path/to/my-app claude mcp add my-app -- openshell mcp --app --start --project /path/to/my-app"},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"Codex / Cursor / other agents","u":"docs/ai.html#codex--cursor--other-agents","x":"Open the app folder – the generated AGENTS.md is picked up automatically. For an MCP connection use {\"command\": \"openshell\", \"args\": [\"mcp\", \"--project\", \"/path/to/my-app\"]} ."},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"Agents without file access to this repository","u":"docs/ai.html#agents-without-file-access-to-this-repository","x":"Point them to llms-full.txt , or let them run openshell guide – the guide ships inside the CLI, so it always matches the installed version."},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"Example prompts","u":"docs/ai.html#example-prompts","x":"Build a desktop app with OpenShell: a time tracker with a Svelte frontend and a Go backend. Projects and time entries are stored locally; AI agents must be able to start and stop timers and get a weekly report. This folder contains a React + FastAPI project. Turn it into an OpenShell desktop app, import the FastAPI routes as agent capabilities and create a Windows installer. Our OpenShell app's backend crashes on startup. Find out why and fix it. (with openshell mcp connected)"},{"t":"OpenShell for AI agents","h":"Writing apps that agents can use well","u":"docs/ai.html#writing-apps-that-agents-can-use-well","x":"One capability per user intention ( add_expense , monthly_summary ), not per database table. Descriptions say what the function does and what it returns; inputs have JSON Schemas with required . Queries get \"permission\": \"read\" , changes \"actions\" – production mode grants exactly these by default. Return JSON objects with ids, so agents can chain calls. Keep UI-only state (filters, selected item) as frontend actions ( registerAction ) with \"permission\": \"ui\" ."},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html","x":"This guide is written for AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Copilot, Aider, custom agents) that are asked to build a new desktop application with OpenShell or to extend an existing OpenShe…"},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"What OpenShell is (in one paragraph)","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#what-openshell-is-in-one-paragraph","x":"OpenShell is a desktop shell (Rust + Tauri 2) that runs a web frontend in a native window and supervises any number of backend processes. An application is not compiled into the shell : it is a normal project folder with a web frontend, one or more services (any language) and an openshell.json that describes them. You write the application; OpenShell starts it, checks health, restarts on crashes, reloads on file changes, collects logs, packages installers and exposes the app to AI agents over MCP."},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"The app contract","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#the-app-contract","x":"Everything an app must do – nothing more: # Rule Why 1 Every service that listens reads its port from the PORT environment variable and binds to 127.0.0.1 . Use \"port\": \"auto\" in the config. Ports are assigned by OpenShell; no conflicts between apps. 2 HTTP services answer GET /health with 200 (configure \"healthcheck\": \"http://127.0.0.1:${port}/health\" ). Readiness and dependency ordering. 3 Persistent data goes to OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR (fallback for running without OpenShell: a local data/ folder). Never write into the project/install folder at runtime. Installed apps live in read-only or replaced folders; the data dir is per user. 4 Log to stdout/stderr , one line per event, unbuffered (Pytho"},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"Workflow","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#workflow","x":""},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"1. Check the environment","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#1-check-the-environment","x":"openshell --version # CLI available? openshell doctor # in a project: config, commands, ports, toolchain openshell create --list # available templates If openshell is missing, tell the user to install it (see the OpenShell README) – do not try to re-implement it."},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"2. Choose the building blocks (new app), a template, or init (existing web app)","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#2-choose-the-building-blocks-new-app-a-template-or-init-existing-web-app","x":"An app needs only the blocks the task requires – frontend, backend, database, functions for AI agents (docs/building-blocks.md). Pick the smallest set: User wants Blocks A UI that stores data locally frontend,database (the page uses OpenShell.db ; no backend) Tools/an AI interface for agents, no UI functions (+ database if data must persist) An API / background service backend (+ database ) A full app frontend,backend,database (+ functions ) openshell create <name> --with frontend,database --lang python # compose; files + openshell.json + AGENTS.md openshell add function <name> # add blocks later, in any order openshell add database [name] | openshell add backend --lang node | openshell add "},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"3. Run it and keep it running","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#3-run-it-and-keep-it-running","x":"openshell dev --headless # no window; prints the URL. Use this when you (the agent) drive the app. openshell dev # with the native window (when the user is watching) Run it in the background (it blocks). Then verify: openshell status # every service must be \"running\" openshell logs --errors # stderr + runtime messages openshell logs backend -n 50 While it runs, OpenShell restarts services whose watch paths change and reloads static frontends – do not restart manually after every edit ; check openshell status / openshell logs instead. Compiled services (Java/C++) rebuild automatically; a failing build shows the service as failed with compiler output in the logs and retries on the next change."},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"4. Implement features","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#4-implement-features","x":"Backend first (endpoint + /health stays green), then frontend, then capabilities. After each step: openshell status , openshell logs --errors , and exercise the endpoint: curl the service URL from openshell status , or call a capability: openshell call cap_<name> '<json>' ."},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"5. Verify the UI like a user","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#5-verify-the-ui-like-a-user","x":"With the app running (window or a browser tab on the printed URL for static frontends): openshell call ui_snapshot # page text + interactive elements with refs (e1, e2, …) openshell call ui_fill '{\"label\": \"Title\", \"value\": \"Test\", \"submit\": true}' openshell call ui_click '{\"text\": \"Save\"}' # or {\"ref\": \"e7\"} openshell call ui_wait_for '{\"text\": \"Saved\"}' openshell call ui_read_text '{\"selector\": \"#list\"}' Notes: the UI bridge is injected into the desktop window and into pages served by OpenShell's static server. For dev server frontends (Vite) in --headless mode it is not injected – verify through the API/capabilities instead, or run openshell dev with the window. If you are connected over "},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"6. Make the app usable for agents (capabilities)","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#6-make-the-app-usable-for-agents-capabilities","x":"Design capabilities like a small, task-oriented API: verbs, clear descriptions, JSON Schema inputs, read for queries and actions for changes. Four ways: 0) Script functions – no server needed ( openshell add function <name> scaffolds one): a script reads a JSON object from stdin and prints JSON to stdout; declare it with \"run\": [\"python\", \"tools/<name>.py\"] . Use the database from scripts through OPENSHELL_DB_<NAME> . Errors: message on stderr + non-zero exit. Logs: openshell logs fn:<name> . a) Declared in openshell.json (HTTP endpoint or task): \"capabilities\": [ { \"name\": \"add_item\", \"description\": \"Create an item. Returns the created item with its id.\", \"permission\": \"actions\", \"input_sch"},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"7. Production check and packaging","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#7-production-check-and-packaging","x":"openshell stop openshell build # frontend.build + services[].build openshell start --headless # production mode (static dist, no file watching, restricted agent permissions) openshell status && openshell stop openshell package # native installer (needs app.version as semver, optional app.icon PNG) openshell package --target bundle # portable .osapp for Android, Termux, servers (any host) openshell release workflow # CI workflow: one tag push builds every platform + download page"},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"openshell.json cheat sheet","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#openshelljson-cheat-sheet","x":"{ \"version\": 1, \"app\": { \"name\": \"Notes\", \"id\": \"com.example.notes\", \"version\": \"0.1.0\", \"description\": \"…\", \"icon\": \"icon.png\" }, \"window\": { \"width\": 1200, \"height\": 800, \"min_width\": 600, \"min_height\": 400 }, // frontend: dev server (command+url), static files (dist) or external url \"frontend\": { \"command\": \"npm run dev -- --host 127.0.0.1 --port ${port} --strictPort\", \"cwd\": \"frontend\", \"port\": \"auto\", \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:${port}\", \"env\": { \"BACKEND_URL\": \"${services.backend.url}\" }, \"setup\": { \"command\": \"npm install\", \"creates\": \"frontend/node_modules\" }, \"build\": \"npm run build\", \"dist\": \"frontend/dist\", // production: OpenShell serves dist \"proxy\": { \"/api\": \"${services.backend."},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"Databases","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#databases","x":"databases: [{ \"name\": \"main\", \"migrations\": \"db/migrations\", \"agent_access\": \"write\" }] gives the app a SQLite file in OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR . Schema changes: add db/migrations/002_….sql (never edit applied migrations; they run once, in order, at startup). Access: services/functions OPENSHELL_DB_MAIN (+ DATABASE_URL ), frontend `OpenShell.db.query / execute (needs actions in agents.sdk_permissions to write), agents db_query / db_execute`. Inspect with openshell call db_list , openshell call db_query '{\"sql\": \"SELECT …\"}' or the console's data browser."},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"Pitfalls","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#pitfalls","x":"Hard-coded ports ( 8000 , 5173 ) → conflicts. Use \"port\": \"auto\" and $PORT . Binding to 0.0.0.0 or localhost → use 127.0.0.1 (the health check and proxy use it; localhost may resolve to ::1 ). Writing data next to the code in a watched folder → endless restarts in dev mode and lost data in installed apps. Use OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR . Buffered output in Java/C++/Python without OpenShell → empty logs. Flush. Vite without --strictPort silently picks another port → the window loads the wrong URL. Absolute URLs to the backend in the frontend → break in production. Use /api + proxy. frontend.dist must exist for production → run openshell build . Installing global tools or changing the user's system is"},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"Definition of done","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#definition-of-done","x":"[ ] Only the building blocks the task needs (no backend just to store data, no UI for a pure agent tool) [ ] openshell doctor shows no errors [ ] openshell dev --headless → openshell status : all services running ; openshell logs --errors clean [ ] Main user flows verified through the API and, where possible, ui_snapshot / ui_click / ui_fill [ ] Capabilities for the app's main functions; each tested with openshell call cap_<name> [ ] Data stored in OPENSHELL_DATA_DIR [ ] Production check: openshell build + openshell start --headless works [ ] README.md of the app explains how to run it ( openshell dev ) and what agents can do [ ] Optional: openshell package produces an installer"},{"t":"Building apps on OpenShell – guide for AI coding agents","h":"Reference","u":"docs/ai-app-development.html#reference","x":"Need Where All config fields openshell schema , docs/configuration.md Agent tools, permissions, MCP docs/agents.md, openshell tools Languages (Java, C++, Go, Rust, .NET) docs/languages.md Installers docs/installers.md Frontend SDK docs/sdk.md, <control_url>/sdk.js HTTP control API docs/control-api.md"},{"t":"Architecture","h":"","u":"docs/architecture.html","x":"OpenShell separates the shell (generic, built once) from the application (frontend, backend, data – owned by the developer and changeable at any time by humans or agents). The shell contains no applic…"},{"t":"Architecture","h":"Components","u":"docs/architecture.html#components","x":"┌──────────────────────────── openshell-desktop (Tauri 2) ─────────────────────────────┐ │ main window ── WebView ── initialization script (window.__OPENSHELL__ + bridge.js) │ │ tray menu · notifications · approval dialogs · loading screen · runtime panel │ │ │ implements `Host` │ │ ┌────────────────────────────── openshell-core ───────────────────────────────────┐ │ │ │ Shell = Runtime + UiHub + Approvals + AuditLog + Policies + control API │ │ │ │ control API (127.0.0.1 only): REST /v1/* · MCP /mcp · WebSocket /v1/ui/bridge │ │ │ │ tool execution (exec.rs) · project-scoped file access (fs.rs) │ │ │ └──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────┘ │ │ │ │ │"},{"t":"Architecture","h":"Key decisions","u":"docs/architecture.html#key-decisions","x":"Decision Rationale Tauri 2 + Rust for the shell Small binaries, system WebView (WebView2/WebKit), cross-platform, strong process control in Rust. Generic shell binary ( openshell-desktop ) instead of generating a Tauri project per app No fork, no per-app Rust code, improvements reach every app. Per-app branding/packaging is a later phase. Services are external processes Any language; code changes never require rebuilding the shell; crash isolation. Supervisor task per service with a desired-state channel ( watch ) Start/stop/restart are just changes of the desired state; the supervisor reconciles. Races (start while stopping) resolve naturally. Ports allocated up front ( \"port\": \"auto\" ), ${"},{"t":"Architecture","h":"Lifecycle of openshell dev","u":"docs/architecture.html#lifecycle-of-openshell-dev","x":"CLI loads and validates openshell.json , checks that no instance runs, launches openshell-desktop --project … --mode development in its own process group. Desktop: Shell::launch builds the plan (ports, URLs, probes), binds the control API, writes .openshell/runtime.json , creates the main window with the loading page and the initialization script. Shell::start sets all autostart services to \"run\". Supervisors wait for dependencies, run setup steps, spawn processes, pipe logs, probe health. When the frontend is healthy the main window navigates to its URL; bridge.js connects back over WebSocket. File changes → backend restart / window reload. Crashes → restart with backoff, failed after N cra"},{"t":"Architecture","h":"Security model (summary)","u":"docs/architecture.html#security-model-summary","x":"Threats considered: other websites in the user's browser (CSRF / DNS rebinding against the loopback API), a frontend page trying to escalate, agents acting outside their remit, path traversal. Not a sandbox against local malware: any local process running as the user can read runtime.json . The permission system is a policy layer for cooperating agents , the audit log provides accountability. See Agent Interaction Layer for details."},{"t":"Platform milestones: Android, universal shell, secure remote access","h":"","u":"docs/platform-milestones.html","x":"This plan splits the platform work (Android, one download per system, apps built on the phone, secure remote access, Play Store) into milestones. Each milestone builds on its own, has tests and is doc…"},{"t":"Platform milestones: Android, universal shell, secure remote access","h":"M1 – Secure remote gateway and exposed services ✅","u":"docs/platform-milestones.html#m1--secure-remote-gateway-and-exposed-services-","x":"Goal: reach the phone's (or any host's) websites, APIs and services from another computer over IP address and port, with security features that can be switched on one by one. Feature Config Default Bind address and port runtime.remote.bind , runtime.remote.port 0.0.0.0:7420 IP / CIDR allowlist runtime.remote.allow everyone who can reach the port Per-device tokens (pairing, QR code) openshell remote pair <device> shared pairing token Rate limit + lockout after failed logins runtime.remote.rate_limit 120 requests/min, 10 failures → 5 min block TLS (own certificate or self-signed with pinned fingerprint) runtime.remote.tls off Opt-in exposure per service (HTTP with token, raw TCP) runtime.remot"},{"t":"Platform milestones: Android, universal shell, secure remote access","h":"M2 – Apps on the phone (Termux) ✅","u":"docs/platform-milestones.html#m2--apps-on-the-phone-termux-","x":"Goal: openshell create , dev , start , package --target bundle on the phone; Python, Node, Java, Rust, Go … from Termux packages run as services, setup and build steps. TermuxAdapter ( crates/runtime/src/platform.rs ): platform android , processes: full , programs from $PREFIX/bin and the project, shell $PREFIX/bin/sh , wake_lock capability. Picked automatically inside Termux; OPENSHELL_ANDROID_MODE=app|termux overrides. openshell doctor gives pkg install … hints per language on Termux. openshell start/dev take a Termux wake lock ( termux-wake-lock ) while services must stay alive."},{"t":"Platform milestones: Android, universal shell, secure remote access","h":"M3 – Universal shell: one link per system ✅ (🚧 Android link)","u":"docs/platform-milestones.html#m3--universal-shell-one-link-per-system---android-link","x":"Goal: a developer builds once; users download one file per system, the program is unchanged. openshell package --target bundle : a portable, platform-neutral app bundle ( <app>-<version>.osapp , a zip with openshell.json and the app files) – runs with openshell start --bundle on any host, including Termux, and is what the Android app imports. openshell package --manifest <base-url> : writes downloads.json (version, file, size, SHA-256 per platform) and a download.html page that picks the visitor's system. openshell package --ci github : writes .github/workflows/openshell-release.yml into the app – one tag push builds Windows, macOS (arm64 + x64), Linux installers and the bundle in parallel a"},{"t":"Platform milestones: Android, universal shell, secure remote access","h":"M4 – Native Android app and Play Store 🚧","u":"docs/platform-milestones.html#m4--native-android-app-and-play-store-","x":"Goal: an Android app (AAB) that can be published in the Play Store. crates/android ( openshell-android , cdylib libopenshell_android.so ): JNI bridge that starts the unchanged core headless with an AndroidAdapter for the APK's native library directory and forwards lifecycle events. apps/android/ – Gradle project (Kotlin): MainActivity (WebView on the app's frontend), RuntimeService (foreground service type specialUse , notification with running services, battery-optimisation request), app bundle import, targetSdk 36 , 16 KB page-size aligned native libraries, release signing from keystore.properties / environment, bundleRelease → AAB. apps/android/store/ – listing texts, data-safety answers,"},{"t":"Platform milestones: Android, universal shell, secure remote access","h":"M5 – Docs, roadmap, changelog ✅","u":"docs/platform-milestones.html#m5--docs-roadmap-changelog-","x":"docs/platforms.md , docs/remote.md , docs/android-app.md , docs/installers.md , docs/configuration.md , docs/cli.md , docs/ai/app-development.md , ROADMAP.md , CHANGELOG.md ."},{"t":"Platform milestones: Android, universal shell, secure remote access","h":"What cannot be finished inside this repository","u":"docs/platform-milestones.html#what-cannot-be-finished-inside-this-repository","x":"Item Why What is prepared Play Store publishing needs the Google Play developer account, an upload key / Play App Signing, the store review (foreground service specialUse justification, data-safety form) AAB build, signing config, listing texts, checklist in docs/android-app.md Signed installers (Windows / macOS) needs certificates, Apple Developer ID and notarisation credentials package.signing + CI secrets Building the Android app locally here no Android SDK/NDK on this machine CI workflow builds it; not verified on a device in this round Bundled Node.js / Python inside the APK needs prebuilt Android runtimes ( libnode.so , libpython.so ) and their licences AndroidAdapter::with_program , O"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"","u":"docs/roadmap.html","x":"Legend: ✅ done · 🚧 partial · ⏳ planned"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"Phase 1 – Foundation ✅","u":"docs/roadmap.html#phase-1--foundation-","x":"✅ Cargo workspace (config, runtime, agent, core, cli, desktop) ✅ Tauri 2 desktop shell with WebView, loading screen, window settings from config ✅ Versioned configuration ( version: 1 ), JSON + TOML, validation with precise paths, JSON Schema"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"Phase 2 – Runtime Manager ✅","u":"docs/roadmap.html#phase-2--runtime-manager-","x":"✅ Process start/stop/restart, process-tree termination, Windows job objects ✅ HTTP / TCP health checks, readiness, unhealthy detection ✅ Restart policies ( never , on-failure , always ), backoff, crash-loop limit ✅ Dependencies and startup order, reverse-order shutdown ✅ Dynamic ports, ${...} variables, external services ✅ Log store (ring buffer + files), log queries ✅ Static frontend server with SPA fallback and reverse proxy"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"Phase 3 – Developer Experience 🚧","u":"docs/roadmap.html#phase-3--developer-experience-","x":"✅ CLI: init, create, dev, start, stop, restart, run, status, logs, doctor, build, schema ✅ Templates: vanilla-python, react-python, react-node, vue-python, svelte-go, vanilla-java, vanilla-cpp, custom ✅ Build steps for compiled services (C++, Java, Go, Rust, .NET), rebuild on change, detection in openshell init ✅ Resource monitoring (CPU/memory per service process tree) in CLI, runtime panel, title bar and tray menus ✅ Development mode with file watching (service restart / window reload) ⏳ Publish CLI binaries (GitHub releases, cargo install , npm wrapper npx openshell ) ✅ Building blocks: openshell add frontend|backend|database|function , openshell create --with … (interactive) ✅ Built-in S"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"Phase 4 – Production 🚧","u":"docs/roadmap.html#phase-4--production-","x":"✅ Production mode, tray, close-to-tray / background mode, headless mode ( --headless , --detach ) ✅ openshell package : per-app installers (NSIS, MSI, DMG, DEB, RPM, AppImage) with name, icon (exe re-branding) and identifier; per-user data directory; single instance; --mcp / --headless modes of installed apps ✅ Code signing settings ( package.signing : Windows thumbprint / sign command, macOS identity + notarisation) ✅ Portable app bundles ( .osapp ), openshell install , release workflow for every platform with downloads.json and a download page ( openshell release ) 🚧 Updates: update check ( package.update_url , GET /v1/update , openshell update ); automatic install ⏳ ⏳ Bundled runtimes (JR"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"Phase 5 – Agent Interaction Layer 🚧","u":"docs/roadmap.html#phase-5--agent-interaction-layer-","x":"✅ Tool catalogue (32 built-in tools), HTTP/JSON API, MCP over stdio and Streamable HTTP ✅ Semantic UI bridge (snapshot, click, fill, press, read, wait, navigate, reload, eval) ✅ Capability discovery (config, OpenAPI import, frontend-registered actions) ✅ App profile (\"use the app\"): only the application's functions as MCP tools; installed apps start headless on demand ✅ Permission scopes per mode, approvals (dialog/CLI/API), audit log ✅ Local provider discovery (Ollama, LM Studio) and configured providers ⏳ Screenshot / vision fallback for canvas-heavy UIs ⏳ Agent sessions with individual permissions and tokens (instead of one agent policy) ⏳ Agent runner integration (hand tasks to a configu"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"Phase 6 – Platforms: Android and connected devices 🚧","u":"docs/roadmap.html#phase-6--platforms-android-and-connected-devices-","x":"✅ Platform-neutral core: RuntimeAdapter (desktop, server, Android), capabilities in status and app_info ✅ services[].platforms , services[].background ( keep / pause ), lifecycle events ( openshell lifecycle , POST /v1/lifecycle ) ✅ Core and CLI build for Android (CI); verified headless on an Android 16 emulator; usable in Termux ✅ Remote gateway ( runtime.remote ): app profile over MCP for other devices, pairing token, openshell remote ✅ Remote security: per-device tokens with QR pairing, IP/CIDR allowlist, rate limit + lockout, TLS (self-signed with pinned fingerprint or own certificate), opt-in exposure of services (HTTP with token login, raw TCP), audit ✅ Termux: TermuxAdapter (toolchain"},{"t":"Roadmap","h":"Phase 7 – Ecosystem ⏳","u":"docs/roadmap.html#phase-7--ecosystem-","x":"🚧 SDK ( @openshell/sdk ) – runtime, window, notifications, agent actions; publish to npm ⏳ Extension system (shell plugins) ⏳ More templates (Next.js, FastAPI with venv, Django, Rails, .NET) ✅ Website: landing page, pages for users, teams, developers and AI agents, download page, searchable documentation ( website/ , scripts/build-website.py ); ⏳ publish it (GitHub Pages or similar) ⏳ Demo video (\"an agent adds a feature to a running desktop app\")"},{"t":"Changelog","h":"","u":"docs/changelog.html","x":"All notable changes to this project are documented here. The format follows Keep a Changelog and the project uses Semantic Versioning."},{"t":"Changelog","h":"[Unreleased]","u":"docs/changelog.html#unreleased","x":""},{"t":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"docs/changelog.html#added","x":"Building blocks : frontend, backend, database and agent functions are independent and freely combinable. openshell add frontend | backend [--lang] | database [name] | function <name> adds a block to an app; openshell create <name> --with frontend,database,… composes a new one (interactive selection in a terminal). Built-in databases ( databases in openshell.json): SQLite files in the app's data directory, migrations, OPENSHELL_DB_<NAME> / DATABASE_URL for services, OpenShell.db.query/execute/schema/list for the frontend, agent tools db_list , db_schema , db_query (read-only), db_execute , per-database agent_access . Script functions : capabilities with \"run\" execute a program per call (JSON "},{"t":"Changelog","h":"[0.2.1] - 2026-11-19","u":"docs/changelog.html#021---2026-11-19","x":""},{"t":"Changelog","h":"Fixed","u":"docs/changelog.html#fixed","x":"HTML5 drag and drop works in the app window: Tauri's drag and drop handler is off, so dragover and drop reach the page (on Windows they did not). Files dropped on the window arrive as in a browser ( dataTransfer.files )."},{"t":"Changelog","h":"[0.2.0] - 2026-11-19","u":"docs/changelog.html#020---2026-11-19","x":""},{"t":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"docs/changelog.html#added-1","x":"window.title_bar: \"overlay\" : the frontend draws its own title bar. Windows and Linux get a window without the system title bar (edges still resize), macOS keeps its window buttons over the content. The desktop window offers window.__OPENSHELL__.window ( minimize , toggleMaximize , close , isMaximized , showMenu , plus title_bar and os ); elements with data-tauri-drag-region move the window. The window permissions are granted at runtime to the app's frontend origin only. OpenShell's loading screen shows a minimal title bar in this mode. The title bar menu (services, reload, window actions) is available on every platform via showMenu() ."},{"t":"Changelog","h":"[0.1.0] - 2026-11-17","u":"docs/changelog.html#010---2026-11-17","x":"First public alpha."},{"t":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"docs/changelog.html#added-2","x":"Tauri 2 desktop shell: window, WebView, loading screen, runtime panel, tray, notifications, approval dialogs, single instance, title bar right-click menu with live service monitoring (Windows). Runtime manager: services in dependency order, setup and build steps, HTTP/TCP health checks, restart policies with backoff, logs, dynamic ports and ${...} variables, static frontend server with reverse proxy, file watching, CPU/memory metrics, process-tree cleanup. Agent Interaction Layer: 28 tools over MCP (stdio and HTTP) and HTTP/JSON, developer and app profiles, semantic UI bridge, capabilities (config, OpenAPI import, frontend actions), permission scopes, approvals, audit log, AI provider discov"},{"t":"Security Policy","h":"","u":"docs/security.html","x":"OpenShell runs processes on the user's machine and gives AI agents controlled access to applications, so security reports are very welcome."},{"t":"Security Policy","h":"Supported versions","u":"docs/security.html#supported-versions","x":"OpenShell is in alpha. Security fixes are made on main and in the latest release."},{"t":"Security Policy","h":"Reporting a vulnerability","u":"docs/security.html#reporting-a-vulnerability","x":"Please do not open a public issue . Report privately through GitHub security advisories with: affected component (runtime, control API, agent layer, UI bridge, installer, …) and version / commit, steps to reproduce or a proof of concept, impact as you understand it. You can expect an acknowledgement within a few days. We will keep you informed and credit you in the release notes unless you prefer otherwise."},{"t":"Security Policy","h":"Scope and threat model","u":"docs/security.html#scope-and-threat-model","x":"In scope, for example: bypassing agent permissions or approvals, escaping the project directory with file tools, reaching the control API from a web page (CSRF, DNS rebinding, Origin bypass), token leaks, injection through configuration that is shipped in installers. The permission layer governs cooperating agents . A local process running as the same user can read .openshell/runtime.json and is out of scope; OpenShell is not a sandbox against local malware. See docs/agents.md#security and Architecture ."},{"t":"Contributing to OpenShell","h":"","u":"docs/contributing.html","x":"Thanks for your interest! OpenShell aims to stay small: a generic shell plus a reliable runtime and agent layer. Please open an issue or discussion before large changes."},{"t":"Contributing to OpenShell","h":"Development setup","u":"docs/contributing.html#development-setup","x":"cargo build # all crates incl. the desktop shell cargo test --workspace --exclude openshell-desktop cargo run -p openshell-cli -- --help Windows: WebView2 (preinstalled on Windows 11). macOS: Xcode command line tools. Linux: libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev libappindicator3-dev librsvg2-dev patchelf (see the Tauri prerequisites). Run the example while developing: cargo build cd examples/todo-app ../../target/debug/openshell dev"},{"t":"Contributing to OpenShell","h":"Where things live","u":"docs/contributing.html#where-things-live","x":"Change Crate Config fields, validation crates/config Process supervision, health, logs, static server crates/runtime Permissions, approvals, audit, UI bridge ( assets/bridge.js ), MCP, tool definitions crates/agent Tool implementations, control API routes crates/core CLI commands, templates crates/cli , templates/ ( templates/_shared is added to every project) Installers crates/package Docs for AI agents docs/ai/ , AGENTS.md , llms.txt Window, tray, dialogs, loading screen apps/desktop Frontend SDK packages/sdk When you add a config field, regenerate the schema: cargo run -p openshell-cli -- schema > schema/openshell.schema.json . When you change documentation, regenerate llms-full.txt : pyt"},{"t":"Contributing to OpenShell","h":"Guidelines","u":"docs/contributing.html#guidelines","x":"Keep the shell technology-agnostic: no assumptions about frontend frameworks, languages or AI vendors. Every agent tool declares exactly one permission scope and is audited. Tests for runtime behaviour use portable shell commands ( echo , exit , ping / sleep ). Run cargo fmt and cargo clippy before opening a PR. Write user-facing docs for new commands, tools and config fields."},{"t":"Contributing to OpenShell","h":"Reporting security issues","u":"docs/contributing.html#reporting-security-issues","x":"Please do not open public issues for vulnerabilities; contact the maintainers privately via GitHub security advisories."},{"t":"For users","h":"","u":"use.html","x":"Install an OpenShell app on Windows, macOS, Linux or Android. Your data stays on your device. Let an AI assistant help, with your approval."},{"t":"For teams and companies","h":"","u":"teams.html","x":"Ship internal tools as signed desktop apps. One download link per system, update checks, audit log, permission scopes, remote access with allowlist and TLS, MIT licence."},{"t":"For developers","h":"","u":"developers.html","x":"Turn a web app into a desktop app. openshell.json, app contract, languages, templates, CLI, SDK, packaging, release workflow."},{"t":"For AI agents","h":"","u":"agents.html","x":"App profile and developer profile over MCP, 32 tools, permission scopes, approvals, UI automation, capabilities, remote gateway, llms.txt."},{"t":"Download","h":"","u":"download.html","x":"Download OpenShell for Windows, macOS, Linux, Android and Termux. Install script, checksums, build from source."}];
